  <h2 id="beginners">Best Leg Workout for Beginners</h2>
  <p>Leg training builds strength for daily movement, supports better posture, improves athletic performance, and helps balance your physique. Strong legs also make cardio, walking, running, and calorie-burning workouts easier.</p>
  <p>A good beginner leg workout should train all major lower-body muscles without overwhelming your joints. Start with bodyweight movements, learn the pattern, then add dumbbells, machines, or resistance bands as your confidence improves.</p>

  <h2>Muscles trained in a complete leg workout</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Quadriceps:</strong> front of the thighs, used heavily in squats, lunges, step-ups, and leg presses.</li>
    <li><strong>Hamstrings:</strong> back of the thighs, trained with Romanian deadlifts, leg curls, hip hinges, and sliding curls.</li>
    <li><strong>Glutes:</strong> powerful hip muscles trained with squats, bridges, lunges, hip thrusts, and deadlift variations.</li>
    <li><strong>Calves:</strong> lower-leg muscles trained with calf raises, jumps, stairs, and loaded carries.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Best leg exercises</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Squats:</strong> great for quads, glutes, and overall lower-body strength.</li>
    <li><strong>Lunges:</strong> improve single-leg strength, balance, and hip stability.</li>
    <li><strong>Romanian deadlifts:</strong> target hamstrings and glutes with a hip-hinge pattern.</li>
    <li><strong>Glute bridges:</strong> simple, joint-friendly glute activation.</li>
    <li><strong>Calf raises:</strong> build calves and ankle strength.</li>
    <li><strong>Leg curls:</strong> isolate the hamstrings and improve knee-flexion strength.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Simple beginner leg workout routine</h2>
  <p>Do 2–3 rounds of this routine 2 times per week. Rest 60–90 seconds between sets and stop if you feel sharp pain.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Bodyweight squats: 10–15 reps</li>
    <li>Reverse lunges: 8–12 reps per leg</li>
    <li>Glute bridges: 12–20 reps</li>
    <li>Wall sits: 20–45 seconds</li>
    <li>Calf raises: 15–25 reps</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Simple gym leg workout routine</h2>
  <p>Use this routine when you have access to machines, dumbbells, or barbells. Keep the first few sessions light so you can learn the movement patterns.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Goblet squat or leg press: 3 sets of 8–12 reps</li>
    <li>Romanian deadlift: 3 sets of 8–12 reps</li>
    <li>Walking lunge: 2–3 sets of 10 reps per leg</li>
    <li>Leg curl machine: 2–4 sets of 10–15 reps</li>
    <li>Seated or standing calf raise: 3 sets of 12–20 reps</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to progress leg workouts</h2>
  <p>Progress one variable at a time. Add a few reps before adding weight, improve your range of motion before increasing load, and keep your technique consistent. If your form breaks down, reduce the weight or take a longer rest.</p>

  <h2>Leg training tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Warm up first:</strong> prepare your hips, knees, and ankles before hard sets.</li>
    <li><strong>Balance your training:</strong> include squats, hinges, hamstring curls, and calf work.</li>
    <li><strong>Control every rep:</strong> smooth reps build more muscle than sloppy reps.</li>
    <li><strong>Progress gradually:</strong> add weight or reps only when your form stays consistent.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Progress safely</h2>
  <p>Start with bodyweight or light resistance. Add reps first, then add weight. Keep your knees tracking over your toes, your core braced, and your back neutral. Stop if you feel sharp pain.</p>
